# ---------------------------------------------------------------- secrets
# The local root's private key. Whoever holds this can issue certificates for
# every permitted domain that your browsers will accept. It must never be
# committed, and there is no legitimate reason to make an exception.
/myroot.key

# Backups written by `rucerts root set-cn` contain plaintext copies of previous
# private keys.
/backup-*/

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- build
/target/

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- generated
# Everything below is rewritten from the certificates on every run, so tracking
# it produces churn and merge conflicts with no benefit.

# The local root certificate. Ignored alongside its key: a certificate whose
# private key is absent cannot sign anything, so committing it would only
# mislead. Run `rucerts init` to create your own.
/myroot.pem

# Cross-certificates, regenerated by `rucerts resign`.
/constrained/

# Installable artifacts. Anchored to the root so that the installer *source*,
# templates/install-certs.ps1, stays tracked.
/install-certs.ps1
/install-certs.cmd
/constrained-ca-policy.reg
/myroot.crt
/*-constrained.crt
/*-original.crt

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- kept
# Deliberately tracked, for the record:
#   rucerts.toml   the permitted-domain list, the meaningful configuration
#   roots/         public CA certificates, the inputs being constrained
#   templates/     the installer source (.ps1) and its .cmd wrapper
#   src/ tests/ Cargo.toml Cargo.lock README.md
#   legacy/        the superseded shell implementation
